"Represents how jointly selected strategies determine each player's payoff in a simultaneous strategic interaction.\n\nUse when: Explore competitive pricing or coordination incentives. Distinguish individual incentives from collectively beneficial outcomes.\n\nAvoid when: The order of moves or information revealed during play is essential.\n\nVisual grammar: Rows and columns enumerate strategies for two named players. Each cell contains an ordered payoff tuple. Labels state whose payoff appears first and how preference is measured.\n\nBuild: Name players, feasible strategies and information assumptions. Specify a consistent payoff convention and evaluate every combination. Inspect best responses while recording uncertainty about actual preferences.\n\nCheck: Each cell includes a payoff for every modeled player. Row and column choices are independently selectable strategies. Any equilibrium claim follows from the stated payoff assumptions.\n\nPitfalls: Treating a cooperative outcome as automatically stable. Inferring real behavior from invented numerical payoffs.\n\nExample: Two vendors choose standard or aggressive discounts; ordered payoff pairs show whether mutual restraint survives each vendor's incentive to deviate.\n\nAlternatives: Game tree, Decision tree."
